# Raijin behind Caddy
#
# Raijin keeps listening on loopback: its default, where no token is needed and nothing is
# exposed to the network. Caddy is the only listener clients reach. Two profiles; Profile B is
# commented out until you fill it in.
#
#   Profile A, local proxy on the Burp machine (plain HTTP on loopback):
#     http://127.0.0.1:9900/mcp (primary MCP), :9901/mcp (secondary MCP), :9902 (dashboard)
#   Profile B, Burp in a VM: HTTPS on the VM address with Caddy's internal CA, a bearer token
#     checked by Caddy for MCP and basic auth for the dashboard:
#     https://<VM-IP>:9443/mcp (MCP), https://<VM-IP>:9444 (dashboard)
#
# Raijin answers "403 invalid Host header" to any Host it does not know (its anti DNS-rebinding
# check), so every upstream rewrites Host to the loopback address Raijin listens on. That rewrite
# hides the Host the client sent, so Caddy has to check it: every site address names its hosts
# (127.0.0.1 and localhost in Profile A, the VM address in Profile B), and a request for any other
# Host, such as a DNS-rebound name (http://rebind.example:9902 resolving to 127.0.0.1), never
# reaches Raijin. Never proxy from a host-less address such as http://:9902: it accepts every Host.
# Raijin's ports default to 9876 (MCP primary), 9877 (MCP secondary) and 9878 (dashboard); change
# the numbers below if you changed mcp_sse_port / mcp_http_port / mcp_dashboard_port.
#
# Install (Linux): sudo cp configs/Caddyfile /etc/caddy/Caddyfile && sudo systemctl reload caddy
# Or run it in the foreground: caddy run --config configs/Caddyfile

# One Raijin listener on loopback. Responses stream (Streamable HTTP, SSE), so nothing is
# buffered and no timeout cuts a long-lived stream.
(raijin) {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:{args[0]} {
		header_up Host 127.0.0.1:{args[0]}
		flush_interval -1
		transport http {
			read_timeout 0
			write_timeout 0
		}
	}
}

# Refuses every request unless it carries "Authorization: Bearer $RAIJIN_TOKEN". Without the
# variable Caddy refuses everything rather than accepting an empty token.
(raijin_bearer) {
	@no_token expression `{env.RAIJIN_TOKEN} == ""`
	respond @no_token "RAIJIN_TOKEN is not set in Caddy's environment" 500
	@unauthorized not header Authorization "Bearer {$RAIJIN_TOKEN}"
	header @unauthorized WWW-Authenticate "Bearer realm=\"raijin\""
	respond @unauthorized "unauthorized" 401
}

# ---- Profile A: local proxy ------------------------------------------------------------------

http://127.0.0.1:9900, http://localhost:9900 {
	bind 127.0.0.1
	import raijin 9876
}

http://127.0.0.1:9901, http://localhost:9901 {
	bind 127.0.0.1
	import raijin 9877
}

http://127.0.0.1:9902, http://localhost:9902 {
	bind 127.0.0.1
	import raijin 9878
}

# Every other Host on these ports gets a 403 from Caddy. Caddy matches host-less addresses last,
# so this block never shadows the named sites above.
http://:9900, http://:9901, http://:9902 {
	bind 127.0.0.1
	respond "invalid Host header" 403
}

# ---- Profile B: Burp in a VM, MCP clients on the host machine ---------------------------------
#
# 1. Replace 192.168.56.10 with the VM address the host reaches (VirtualBox / VMware host-only,
#    LAN, or the Tailscale / WireGuard IP). Raijin itself stays on 127.0.0.1.
# 2. Give Caddy the token in its environment, e.g. with `sudo systemctl edit caddy`:
#        [Service]
#        Environment=RAIJIN_TOKEN=<long random string, e.g. from: openssl rand -hex 32>
#    Using the token from Raijin's Server tab lets you also tick "Require Auth Token" in Raijin,
#    so both layers check the same header.
# 3. Create the dashboard password hash: caddy hash-password --plaintext '<password>'
#    (Caddy older than 2.8 spells the directive `basicauth`).
# 4. On the host machine, trust Caddy's local CA: copy root.crt from Caddy's data directory
#    (caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt) and point the client at it, for example
#    NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS=/path/to/root.crt for Node-based MCP clients.
# 5. Client config: {"type": "http", "url": "https://192.168.56.10:9443/mcp",
#                    "headers": {"Authorization": "Bearer <RAIJIN_TOKEN>"}}
#
# https://192.168.56.10:9443 {
# 	bind 192.168.56.10
# 	tls internal
# 	import raijin_bearer
# 	import raijin 9876
# }
#
# https://192.168.56.10:9444 {
# 	bind 192.168.56.10
# 	tls internal
# 	basic_auth {
# 		raijin <bcrypt hash from step 3>
# 	}
# 	import raijin 9878
# }
